I've never heard of any of those languages, except Ghotuo which I found whe=
n=20
looking at template aaa on Wiktionary, and I think that if you run this=20
nunfu'ivlazba to completion, you're going to end up with a humongous list=
=20
that's as desuet as a dule of doves.

The way language names are coined in natural languages is completely=20
different. People who are interested in a particular language (hereinafter=
=20
Pelonian) find out what the Pelonians call their language (which requires=
=20
learning some Pelonian) and what their neighbors the Almonians call it. The=
n=20
they decide what to call it in, say, English. Then they write reports about=
=20
Pelonian and other people learn that there is such a language. They may, on=
=20
learning more Pelonian, figure out that they used the wrong word to name th=
e=20
language ("Auca", for instance, is an exonym meaning "enemy", so they are n=
ow=20
called "Waorani" or other spellings thereof).

I do not see a need for coining a type-4 or even type-3 fu'ivla for Baatonu=
m=20
or Babatana until some Lojbanist is interested in those languages. Leave th=
em=20
at type 1.

